DOMAIN_OVERVIEW:
The Theory of Communicative Action (1981/1984–1987) is the theoretical heart
of Habermas' philosophy of history and his alternative to speculative
philosophy of history. Volume 1 reconstructs the concept of communicative
rationality from Mead, Durkheim, Parsons, and Weber; Volume 2 develops the
two-level concept of society (system and lifeworld) and the colonization
thesis. The formal-pragmatic theory of validity claims — truth, normative
rightness, truthfulness — grounds a proceduralist account of rational
discourse that can serve as the normative standard for historical critique
without reverting to teleological metaphysics.

The reconstructive method distinguishes this project from transcendental
philosophy on one side and empirical social science on the other. It
rationally reconstructs the implicit competences that speakers must
exercise in communicative action, treating these as universal (if
fallibilist) achievements of communicative rationality.

RELEVANCE_TO_PROJECT:
This domain provides the philosophical apparatus through which Habermas
articulates his alternative to speculative philosophy of history. The
two-level concept of society (system/lifeworld) gives him a framework for
historical analysis that tracks both functional integration (system) and
social integration (lifeworld) without reducing one to the other. The
colonization thesis is his concrete historical-diagnostic claim about
modernity. Without this domain, the review cannot explain how Habermas'
alternative to speculative philosophy of history actually works.

SYNTHESIS_GUIDANCE:
Central tension: the reconstructive method claims universality (the validity
claims are supposed to be transcultural) while the empirical content of the
theory (the lifeworld, the colonization thesis) is drawn from a particular
historical experience. Critics (Owen 1998, Strydom 2014) press on whether
the developmental logic is genuinely universal or merely Eurocentric.
Synthesis should track how Habermas' methodological claims relate to the
historical scope of his theory.

KEY_POSITIONS:
- Primary sources: 3 entries — TCA vol 1, TCA vol 2, Universal Pragmatics
- Critical introduction/reconstruction: 3 papers — Thompson, Niemi ×2
- Colonization and lifeworld: 2 papers — Brand, Condon
- Critical challenges (Eurocentrism, cognitive sociology): 2 papers — Owen, Strydom
- Empirical/experimental: 1 paper — Schaefer & Heinze
====================================================================
}

