  doi = {10.1057/978-1-137-04505-8_4},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: The text of Habermas' 1980 Adorno Prize lecture, which coined
  the phrase "modernity as an unfinished project." Habermas argues that the
  differentiation of reason into science, morality, and art — the cultural
  modernity diagnosed by Weber — has not been translated into the communicative
  practice of everyday life. The task is not to abandon the Enlightenment
  project but to reconnect these differentiated spheres to the lifeworld.
  The lecture explicitly positions this against neoconservative and
  postmodern attempts to declare modernity over.

  RELEVANCE: The founding document of Habermas' "unfinished project" thesis.
